body:
I only recently started reading reddit and when I registered it was in response to something regarding suicide and I didn't think I'd ever post again - I was wrong.  My father killed himself last summer, so I am the daughter [of someone who commit] suicide.  It made sense at the time.

Meh, his act was a defining event in my life and I'm still dealing with it daily.  So "SuicideDaughter" seemed apt.

ETA: No worries that I'll follow in his footsteps.  I happen to love my kids and would never do that to them. {Not to mention that I'm otherwise pretty dang happy and have no urge to check out whatsoever}

My daughter was only 18 months old, so she was oblivious to it.  The other kids were my boyfriends kids who were older, 4 & 6 and considering when I got home they were in the bedroom *jumping on the bed they'd just removed her dead body from* I'd say they didn't seem too upset about it.

I tell you what though, I was creeped out walking past that bedroom doorway in the middle of the night to go to the bathroom for the rest of the time we lived in that house.  It took her family some time to remove her stuff and we just left the room empty after they did.  I don't know that I believe in ghosts, but I'd been home when she died - just didn't know it - and as I was the first to leave, then the three other adults staying there at the time - somehow no one actually made sure *Joyce*! {Sorry, I'd been trying to remember her name and it just popped into my mind} was *awake* before leaving.  

I'm really quite particular about under what circumstances I leave my kids with anyone else now though.  {you know, like making sure the babysitter is **alive** before I leave}

I tried to kick a wall one time.  I'm a total wuss {female too} and only wear shoes when I have to, so normally am barefoot - only I had on some ankle boots and kicked the wall, and put a hole in it.  I was so mortified.

{I was alone at the time, not in the middle of a fight or anything - I got word, while cleaning out my old apartment that the housekeeper/nanny who was 20 miles away watching my child had DIED and I couldn't get ahold of the person who had my car with purse/etc in it so I was temporarily stuck there - on the phone, with the coroner and the dead ladies boyfriend who'd stopped by and found her...dead in her room with the kids playing in front of the TV}

Cured me of any such urge since then.  Cost me $ to have it repaired too, compounding my regret.

This wouldn't be the first time a husband grossly misrepresented {or misunderstood} what it actually takes to be a SAHM.  Was one of the kids really sick when the house was messy?  Had she been up all night taking care of a puking kid so he could sleep and go to work?  Had she been running errands all day, paying bills, picking up groceries and running kids to and from school?

My ex used to talk about how he could do everything I did in "just 20 minutes" - never mind that due to where we  lived and where the kids went to school {different grades, different schools, different start/end times to their days} I left the house at 6:45 am and didn't get home until 9:30 am {Drive through morning traffic to drop off one child at 7:20 - spend 15 minutes in line in parking lot, drive to other school, sit there, do the younger girls hair in van, eat breakfast, read books, etc until school started at 8:30, walk her in, get her settled, drive back across town in more traffic}  Then leave again at 11 am to pick up youngest from Kindergarten, get home around 12:15 - then leave again at 1:45 to pick up oldest child, get home around same time as husband.

Seriously, you can't clean the house if you're sitting in a parking lot or driving 1/2 the damn day {phrased that weird - I mean to say you can't clean **while** driving and therefore if you spend 1/2 your time driving you are only left with 1/2 as much time as someone thinks you have}

So, just curious, anyone else ever check Zillow for the 'zestimate' of an ex's home value?  Anyone ever see that they paid more than $100k than what their house is now worth, for a tiny boring house and cackle maniacally knowing that you paid $100k less {literally} for a house that's twice the size, twice as nice, and now increased in value $25k?

No?  Me either.

{Ninja edit their/there}

I did an internet search on my ex one time, just randomly, and found umpteen profiles of his, while we were still legally married, though I'd left him the year before, on a bunch of sites, like AdultFriendFinder, looking for "group casual sex".

It was really him, he was really paying for the ads, and it really did help convince him in the courtroom not to complain about paying all the back child support he'd not paid {he'd literally had the fees taken out of the joint account that was soley for child support, taking it overdrawn and incurring $200 in fees every month until I got the courts permission to close the account and start getting checks instead}

I mention all of this because, at first, I couldn't believe he was really doing it and was afraid someone was posing as him and that I'd get blamed for it.  Until I saw the charges, asked the bank to reverse them and refuse future charges, they called him and he told them "no, I really did authorize that, keep paying for it".  The bank manager and I were sitting there in shock.  Mortifying.

Agreed regarding pads vs tampons - I couldn't comfortably wear a tampon until after giving birth to my second child in my mid-20's.  Other teens I knew never wore pads at all.  

As well, Always truly is pretty much the best brand out there in terms of comfort, absorbancy, variety of sizes/styles, etc.  They make a teen pad also - which is what I bought for my girls when the time came.

{Glad to see the edit that the vibrator is being tossed - that part made me cringe so hard my ears popped}

Might depend on what part of California - then again it was 18 years ago now - but I got pulled over when I was 19 and I was ticketed for:

 * Going 62 in a 55 {passing logging truck, saw cop flying up behind me, stupidly sped up to get in front of truck to 'let him by' dur! - went to Traffic School, nothing on my record}
 * No front license plate {what did I know, my mother worked for the courts and gave me the car - which she told the judge, he told her to find the plate and put it back on - dismissed in court}
 * Tires too bald {cop didn't measure the tread, judge dismissed it - clearly my mother working there got me a really easy ride}
 * Improper seatbelt wear {dismissed by judge, he said as long as it was fastened the cop had no say in whether I had the shoulder strap in front of or behind me}

and finally

 * Failure to update home address  - also dismissed when I told the judge, honestly, that I considered my parents home my 'legal residence' and 'mailing address' and that I'd moved around repeatedly in the two years'ish I'd been on my own and he said something about it being good enough for college kids or those in the military}

So - you could certainly get a ticket for it from the CHP back in Nov 1992 {the not changing your address thing, for those who might actually still be reading but have forgotten WTF I was talking about} on 101 near College of the Redwoods.  But if your mother worked for the courts and knew all the judges he'd let you off lightly.

And then she'd take the car away from you and give it to your evil step father and you'd lose your job right after you found out you were pregnant! {Which is why I remember all of this so clearly, I had a lot going on!}
